      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})+2,\\,y=1\\sin(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-1,\\,\\pi\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-18\\sin(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3\\cos(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3\\cos(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{17\\pi}{12}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{12}) & =-18\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{12}) & =18\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{4}) & =-18\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{25\\pi}{12}) & =18\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{17\\pi}{12}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
